{"attachments":[],"author":{"display_name":"Nobleindie","id":"361351593292988425","is_bot":0,"username":"nobleindie"},"author_id":"361351593292988425","channel_id":"863743873411317800","content":"<@!150319814836092928> \nVivica left a note for you in response to the one you left for Vivica \n```I can only assume this means you've told him, of which I am so very excited and happy for you. Your question about marriage has multiple answers. Some see it as a way to formally and legally say you will be committed to someone for the rest of their lives, Ceremony does a similar thing except it cannot be cast on someone again until widowed, meaning their SO has died. Some see marriage as a way to simply publicly announce your commitment to someone as your partner. As for what you do during a marriage ceremony, we'll that's up to you. Many follow family or cultural traditions, some do something symbolic to represent their union. If you would like to discuss this more let me know. -Vivica ```","created_at":"2021-08-20T05:48:28.426000Z","edited_at":null,"id":"878153494643748915","reply_to_id":null,"thread":{"archived":1,"channel_id":"863743873411317800","created_at":"2021-08-18T16:26:08.418000Z","id":"877589192950046730","name":"Notes and information"},"thread_id":"877589192950046730"}
